              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p Where
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
              is
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
              regarded as the
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+toposes"} elementary toposes
          %p so
          %ul
            %li
              2-categorical logic, or
              %em 2-logic
              for short, as understood in this entry here, is (or should be, once developed) the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
              of this situation, namely the study of the
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} language
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-dimensional+categories"} two-dimensional categories
              (understood in the generality including
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategories"} bicategories
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+categories"} virtual double categories
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  elementary
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-toposes"} 2-toposes
          %p
            Therefore, the step from
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            to formal 2-logic/2-categorical logic is analogous to the step from the former to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                \-categories
          %p
            This means that 2-logic is a genuine extension of ordinary logic with new logical constructions and phenomena (largely still to be isolated properly) — analogous to how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            enhances ordinary logical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            = succeed "-types" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+categories"} small categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-types"}
              homotopy
              = succeed "-types" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,0)
            also known as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+0-types"} 0-types
            also known as
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
          %p For example:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                where
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifiers"} subobject classifiers
                in ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+toposes"} elementary toposes
                serve as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universes+of+propositions"} universes of propositions
                for their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+logic"} first order
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            %li
              %p
                where
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifiers"} object classifiers
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-toposes"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                  \-toposes
                serve as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
                for their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            %li
              %p
                so
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-toposes"} 2-toposes
                have
                %em fibration classifiers
                which should serve as universes of “2-types” for their internal 2-logic.
              %p
                Accordingly,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/powersets"} powersets
                in ordinary logic/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
                become
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+presheaves"} categories of presheaves
                in 2-logic, and the Cantor embedding becomes the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
          %p
            In fact, there should be a joint generalization and unification of 2-logic of “2-types” with the “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            \-logic” of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C2%29-categories"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,2)
              \-categories
            (notably of
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C2%29-toposes"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,2)
                \-toposes
            = succeed "-logic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,2)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+type+theory"} directed homotopy type theory
            and as such, even if itself nascent, may be the most developed form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntactic
            2-logic that has found attention so far.
          %p
            This means that 2-logic in the sense of this page here is
            %em not
            about the related but different idea of using general tools from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theory
            in the study of ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            %em not
            along the lines of how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+category+theory"} formal category theory
            uses
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theory
            to study
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-categories"} 1-categories
          %p Of course, there may be relation and overlap between 2-logic proper and such “formal categorical logic” using 2-categorical methods in 1-logic. With genuine 2-logic not being developed much at all at this point, most of the references listed below actually fall on the side of 2-categorical methods in 1-logic.
          %p
            For instance, for a suitable 2-dimensional
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sketch"} sketch
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            is a category of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} 1-theories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+categories"} coherent categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+categories"} regular categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lex+categories"} lex categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monads"} 2-monads
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lex"} Lex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+semantics"} functorial semantics
            = succeed "." do
              %em 2-theories are logics
            = succeed "." do
              %em 2-models are 1-theories
